---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GovernedTransactionLoop from "../components/diagrams/GovernedTransactionLoop.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Canonical Workflow | Complete Governed AI Transaction"
  description="BeaconGuard’s complete governed AI transaction: Request Admission, provider invocation, untrusted AI result, Result-to-Action Authorization, enterprise execution, system-of-record effect, and verification."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Complete governed AI transaction</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This is the authoritative reusable workflow. BeaconGuard authorizes whether
      an enterprise application may invoke an AI provider and whether a declared
      enterprise action based on the returned result may proceed. AI output
      remains an untrusted proposal. BeaconGuard authorizes; the enterprise
      application executes; systems of record remain authoritative.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Authoritative transaction loop</h2>
    <p>
      The complete sequence separates provider invocation from enterprise
      effect and returns authority to the same enterprise application before
      system-of-record change.
    </p>
    <GovernedTransactionLoop
      variant="complete"
      title="Authoritative governed transaction loop"
      caption="Complete closed-loop transaction with dual DENY hard stops, evidence rail, and system-of-record authority."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Separated stages</h2>
    <div class="scenario-grid">
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Provider invocation</h3>
        <p>
          After Request Admission ALLOW, the enterprise application may invoke
          the AI provider under the Active Verified Release. DENY blocks
          invocation and records the decision.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Untrusted result</h3>
        <p>
          The AI-generated result is an untrusted proposal. Provider success is
          not enterprise authorization and does not grant permission to change
          enterprise state.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Declared action</h3>
        <p>
          The enterprise application declares the intended enterprise action and
          submits it for Result-to-Action Authorization.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Application execution</h3>
        <p>
          Only after action ALLOW may the same application execute the
          authorized action. DENY hard-stops enterprise effect.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>System of record</h3>
        <p>
          The enterprise system of record remains authoritative for resulting
          business state. BeaconGuard does not become that system of record.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="scenario-card">
        <h3>Evidence and Verify</h3>
        <p>
          Related Verifiable Decision Records support investigation through
          BeaconGuard Verify. Verify is read-only and cannot grant runtime
          authority.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Where this applies</h2>
    <p>
      The same governed transaction applies across priority regulated workflows.
      Industry pages describe illustrative scenarios — not locked commercial
      markets or customer production validation.
    </p>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/how-it-works">See How It Works</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/healthcare">Healthcare workflows</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/fintech">Financial Services workflows</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/use-cases">Use cases</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
